WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.092 --> 00:00:10.375
Narrator: Andy is a furniture refinisher

00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:13.600
who runs the YouTube
channel Modern Makeovers.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.730
This dresser was made in the 1920s.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.470
Over years of use, the veneer
is starting to peel off.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.040
There are marker drawings on it,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.230
and the stain is starting to age.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.030
Andy shows us how he restores this dresser

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.450
by removing the old veneer

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.000
and replacing it with new veneer strips,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.740
removing the old stain
and applying a new coat,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.600
repairing the handles,
and applying a topcoat

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.506
to seal in all his work.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.250
The veneer on this dresser

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.417
is severely damaged and unsalvageable.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.411
Andy is using a heat gun

00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:53.527
and a putty knife to strip it off.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.760
The heat softens the
glue between the veneer

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.140
and the dresser, so
that the wood underneath

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.273
won't get damaged as he strips it.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.893
He sands the whole
piece down to bare wood.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.270
This makes it easier
for the stain to absorb

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.770
when he applies it
later on in the process.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.508
Now he's applying a paint stripper

00:01:28.508 --> 00:01:31.233
to the sides of the dresser
to remove the old stain.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.140
It sits for 15 minutes,

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.653
then he scrubs the area with steel wool.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.533
Andy is replacing the old veneer

00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:48.567
with veneer edge bandings.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:57.820
He arranges the strips,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.303
carefully considering the width,

00:01:59.303 --> 00:02:01.513
grain pattern, and color of each.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.480
Once he's happy with how they look,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.920
he numbers them to make sure

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.263
he can put them back in the same order.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.680
Andy is covering the surrounding
areas with painter's tape

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.245
in order to protect them.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.220
He applies a generous
amount of contact cement

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.853
to the strips with a roller

00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:28.628
as well as the section of the dresser

00:02:28.628 --> 00:02:30.280
he'll be applying the strips to

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.210
in order to get a strong adhesion.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.260
Andy waits 15 minutes and
then does a touch test.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.920
If the contact cement
isn't tacky to the touch,

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.737
the strips are ready to go on.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.090
He is applying one strip at a time

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.963
to make sure the application is perfect.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.710
Then, using a wooden
stick with a rounded edge,

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.920
he presses down hard
across the entire surface

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.983
to make sure it is fully adhered.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.330
He repeats this process
one strip at a time,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.153
until the entire top of
the dresser is covered.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.110
Andy is breaking off the
excess length of the strips

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.269
and sanding the edges smooth.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.065
To give the front edge of
the veneer a cleaner finish,

00:03:24.065 --> 00:03:25.810
he adds a thin strip of wood

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.251
to blanket the edges of the veneer strips.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.230
Andy is doing this by cutting a piece

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.200
of scrap wood with a table saw

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.791
and applying it to the
dresser with wood glue.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.363
He is restaining the body of
the piece with an oil stain.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.760
Andy slathers the stain on with a brush

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.027
and then wipes it away with a towel.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.950
He is removing the
handles from the drawers

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.853
so he can work on them separately.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.640
Andy sands the existing
finish on the drawers

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.981
in order to apply a new finish.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:19.723
He is applying paint stripper
to the handles using a brush.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.890
After 15 minutes, he can scrub
away the stain and finish

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:30.506
on the handles using a scratch brush.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.660
Andy is staining the handles

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.283
using a water-based stain in black.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.283
He applies the stain
using a pair of socks.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.960
Cotton is lint-free and highly absorbent,

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.635
making it a perfect tool for staining.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.750
He uses a water-based
topcoat to seal the drawers

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.408
and the body of the dresser.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.500
After years of use, this
peeling dresser is now

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.308
a fully repaired piece that
is ready to be used again.

